    "narrative": "Our offensive output today hinges on the heavy lifting from **Cal Raleigh** (78.97) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (66.07), who represent our highest-ceiling plays against favorable pitching environments. **Cal Raleigh** benefits significantly from a high-yield matchup against Sugano; despite a minor platoon disadvantage, the park factors and the pitcher\u2019s sub-par xERA elevate him to our premier start. Meanwhile, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a locked-in asset, gaining a strategic bump from his tendency to capitalize on slower SP deliveries\u2014a vital metric for our pursuit of stolen bases, where his efficiency against this specific arm provides a clear path to production. **Bryce Harper** (64.58) and **JJ Bleday** (63.38) round out our core; **JJ Bleday** specifically is a standout play today, as his dynamic platoon advantage against Peterson allows him to transcend his base projection and act as a reliable anchor in the OF5 slot.\n\nThe optimizer faced a difficult choice regarding the final bench spot, with **Zack Gelof** (39.57) ultimately falling just shy of the 40-point floor. While **Zack Gelof** has demonstrated upside, the combination of a tough matchup against Williams and a negative platoon split (R/R) forced him to the bench in favor of our active starters. We are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Jeremiah Jackson** (51.65) and **Dylan Beavers** (55.80); both players face an elite arm in Misiorowski, but their projected spot at the top of the order and park-adjusted scoring keep them in the lineup. We must be cautious with these two, as Misiorowski\u2019s 2.32 xERA is a significant threat to our efficiency, and any official lineup changes dropping them down the order would necessitate an immediate re-evaluation of their utility.",
